.TH ZERODDS.YAML 5 "" "ZeroDDS" "ZeroDDS Manual"
.SH NAME
zerodds.yaml \- the ZeroDDS runtime configuration
.SH SYNOPSIS
.I /etc/zerodds/zerodds.yaml
.br
.I /etc/zerodds/zerodds.d/*.yaml
.br
.I /run/zerodds/zerodds.d/*.yaml
.SH DESCRIPTION
Every ZeroDDS program reads the settings of its DDS runtime from four
layers; a later layer wins:
.IP 1. 4
the built-in defaults;
.IP 2. 4
the environment, under the
.B ZERODDS_*
name of each key (below);
.IP 3. 4
the files: without
.B \-\-config
the system directory
.RI ( /etc/zerodds
on Linux,
.I /usr/local/etc/zerodds
on macOS,
.I %PROGRAMDATA%\eZeroDDS
on Windows) with
.IR zerodds.yaml ,
then the files of
.I zerodds.d/
in name order, on Linux last those of
.IR /run/zerodds/zerodds.d ;
with
.B \-\-config
.I FILE
that file, which must exist, and the directory
.IR FILE.d/ ;
.IP 4. 4
.B \-\-set
.IR path = value
on the command line, repeatable, in the given order;
.B \-\-set
.RI + path = value
appends to a list.
.PP
A bridge daemon reads its own file
.RI ( ws\-bridged.yaml
and so on) instead of
.IR zerodds.yaml ;
these keys may stand in that file and configure the daemon's DDS side.
A missing implicit file is not an
error. An invalid value, an unreadable file or a failed include stops the
program; the message names the layer (file and line,
.BR $VARIABLE ,
or the
.BR \-\-set ).
An unknown key in a file is a warning at start and an error under
.BR \-\-check\-config ,
which prints every value with its origin and exits 0 (valid), 1 (invalid
value or unknown key) or 2 (unreadable file, syntax error, include cycle).
.PP
The reader is
.I RuntimeConfig::from_source
in
.IR crates/dcps/src/runtime.rs ;
the table
.I SETTINGS
there pairs every key with its environment variable.
.SH SYNTAX
A subset of YAML: maps, block lists
.RI ( "\- a" ),
flat inline lists of scalars
.RI ( "[a, \(dqb\(dq]" ),
scalars, comments. Inline maps and nested inline lists are refused.
.PP
.BI include: " PATH"
or a list of paths loads further files or directories, relative to the
including file, before the file's own values; nesting is allowed up to 16
levels, a cycle is refused.
.PP
Maps merge across files; a list replaces the list below it, a key
.BI + name
appends to it. A list from the environment is a comma list.
.PP
.BI ${VAR} " and " ${VAR:-default}
are substituted from the environment before a document is parsed.
.PP
Booleans are
.BR true / false
or
.BR 1 / 0 .
.SH KEYS
Each entry names the default and, in brackets, the environment variable.
.SS runtime
.TP
.B runtime.tick_period_ms
Period of the runtime tick (heartbeats, timers), ms, > 0. Default 5.
.RB [ ZERODDS_TICK_PERIOD_MS ]
.TP
.B runtime.scheduler_tick
The event-driven deadline scheduler;
.B false
runs a fixed tick loop. Default true.
.RB [ ZERODDS_SCHEDULER_TICK ]
.SS transport
.TP
.B transport.tcp_locator_kinds
.BR vendor " or " fast-dds :
the LocatorKind values of TCP locators. vendor sends ZeroDDS's own kinds in the
vendor range of DDSI-RTPS 2.5 \(sc9.3.2.4 (0x01F0_0004, 0x01F0_0005); fast-dds sends 4 and 8, the
values of Fast DDS, for TCP interoperation with it. Reading accepts both. Default vendor.
.RB [ ZERODDS_TCP_LOCATOR_KINDS ]
.TP
.B transport.max_message_size
Largest RTPS message sent, bytes, at least the MTU. Default 14720.
.RB [ ZERODDS_MAX_MESSAGE_SIZE ]
.TP
.B transport.shm_max_datagram
Datagram limit of the same-host SHM transport, bytes, > 0. Default 65536.
.RB [ ZERODDS_SHM_MAX_DATAGRAM ]
.TP
.B transport.same_host_shm
Whether a writer and a reader on the same host exchange user samples through
a shared-memory segment;
.B false
sends every such pair over the user transport. Default true in a build
with the same-host SHM feature and false without it;
.B true
in a build without it makes the runtime refuse to start.
.RB [ ZERODDS_SAME_HOST_SHM ]
.TP
.B transport.user_multicast
Join the SPDP multicast group on the user-traffic multicast port of DDSI-RTPS
2.5 \(sc9.6.2.3 (PB + DG * domainId + d2), receive user traffic there and
announce the locator as the participant's default multicast locator. A peer may
then send user samples by multicast. ZeroDDS writers send to the unicast
locators of a peer only. A refused join is logged and the participant runs
without it. Set false for a host without multicast. Default true.
.RB [ ZERODDS_USER_MULTICAST ]
.TP
.B transport.recv_batch
Datagrams per batched receive call, > 0;
.B 1
is one
.BR recv ()
per datagram. Default 32.
.RB [ ZERODDS_RECV_BATCH ]
.TP
.B transport.batch_at_zero_budget
Batch also at a latency budget of 0. Default false.
.RB [ ZERODDS_BATCH_AT_ZERO_BUDGET ]
.TP
.B transport.interface
IPv4 address of the interface to announce and bind; unset, a route probe
chooses. Default unset.
.RB [ ZERODDS_INTERFACE ]
.TP
.B transport.user_transport
Transport of the user data:
.BR UDPv4 ", " UDPv6 ", " TCPv4 ", " TCPv6 ;
.BR SHM ", " UDS ", " TSN
in a build with the matching feature. Discovery stays on UDPv4. Default
UDPv4.
.RB [ ZERODDS_USER_TRANSPORT ]
.TP
.B transport.user_transports
List of the same values, the first preferred; a non-empty list replaces
.BR transport.user_transport .
Default empty.
.RB [ ZERODDS_USER_TRANSPORTS ]
.TP
.B transport.udp.connected_cache
Keep a connected send socket per destination;
.B false
sends with
.BR sendto ().
Default true.
.RB [ ZERODDS_UDP_CACHE_ENABLE ]
.TP
.B transport.udp.cache_max_entries
Entries of that cache, > 0, oldest evicted first. Default 16.
.RB [ ZERODDS_UDP_CACHE_MAX ]
.TP
.B transport.udp.cache_sndbuf
.B SO_SNDBUF
of the cached sockets, bytes; 0 keeps the kernel default. Default 262144.
.RB [ ZERODDS_UDP_CACHE_SNDBUF ]
.TP
.B transport.udp.recv_buffer
.B SO_RCVBUF
of every bound UDP socket, bytes; 0 keeps the kernel default. Default
1048576.
.RB [ ZERODDS_UDP_RCVBUF ]
.TP
.B transport.udp.dont_route
.BR SO_DONTROUTE ;
only for peers on the local subnet. Default false.
.RB [ ZERODDS_UDP_DONTROUTE ]
.TP
.B transport.udp.max_message_size
Largest RTPS message the UDP transport sends in one datagram, bytes, 20 to
65507. The fragment size of a writer follows the smallest such size of its
transports (DDSI-RTPS 2.5 \(sc8.4.14.1): size less 128 octets, so 1344 at
the default. Default 1472, a UDP datagram that fits an Ethernet frame.
.RB [ ZERODDS_UDP_MAX_MESSAGE_SIZE ]
.TP
.B transport.interface_bindings
File only, a list of maps with
.B name
and
.B bind
.RI ( ip " or " ip:port ),
.B kind
.RB ( loopback ", " localhost ", " wan ", " subnet ", "
.BI named: class\fR),
.B subnet
(CIDR) and optionally
.BR default ;
the interfaces that
.I NetInterface
rules of DDS Security bind to. Needs a build with the
.B security
feature. Default empty.
.SS reader
.TP
.B reader.max_samples_per_proxy
Samples a reader buffers per remote writer across a gap, > 0. Default 65536.
.RB [ ZERODDS_MAX_SAMPLES_PER_PROXY ]
.TP
.B reader.max_reassembly_sample_bytes
Largest sample reassembled from fragments, bytes, > 0. Default 16777216.
.RB [ ZERODDS_MAX_SAMPLE_BYTES ]
.TP
.B reader.time_based_filter_clock
The clock a reader's TIME_BASED_FILTER measures minimum_separation on; DDS
1.4 \(sc2.2.3.12 leaves it open.
.B sender
takes the source timestamp of the change (INFO_TS), the reception time for a
sample without one;
.B receiver
takes the reception time. Default sender.
.RB [ ZERODDS_READER_TIME_BASED_FILTER_CLOCK ]
.TP
.B reader.heartbeat_response_delay_ms
How long a reliable reader waits after a HEARTBEAT before it answers, ms,
>= 0 (DDSI-RTPS 2.5 \(sc8.4.10.1 heartbeatResponseDelay). Default 500, the
specification's; 0 answers on the next tick.
.RB [ ZERODDS_READER_HEARTBEAT_RESPONSE_DELAY_MS ]
.TP
.B reader.heartbeat_suppression_duration_ms
A HEARTBEAT that reaches a reliable reader less than this long after the
previous one of its writer is ignored, ms, >= 0 (DDSI-RTPS 2.5 \(sc8.4.10.1
heartbeatSuppressionDuration). Default 0, the specification's value.
.RB [ ZERODDS_READER_HEARTBEAT_SUPPRESSION_DURATION_MS ]
.TP
.B reader.honor_data_max_size_serialized
Whether a reader refuses a SerializedPayload larger than the
dataMaxSizeSerialized the matched writer announced (DDSI-RTPS 2.5
\(sc8.4.10.4, Table 8.72).
.B false
takes it, for a writer that exceeds its own announcement (Fast DDS 3.6.1:
160 octets announced, 100 036 sent, OMG dds-rtps Test_LargeData_0). Default
true.
.RB [ ZERODDS_READER_HONOR_DATA_MAX_SIZE_SERIALIZED ]
.TP
.B reader.dispose_with_unregister
Whether a reader takes a lifecycle marker with the DisposedFlag and the
UnregisteredFlag of PID_STATUS_INFO (DDSI-RTPS 2.5 \(sc9.6.3.9) as a dispose
and an unregister.
.B false
takes it as an unregister only: the instance goes to NOT_ALIVE_NO_WRITERS
once its last writer is gone (OMG dds-rtps Test_FinalInstanceState_2 with a
publisher that disposes and unregisters in one marker when its writer is
deleted, measured OpenDDS 3.35.0-dev). Default true.
.RB [ ZERODDS_READER_DISPOSE_WITH_UNREGISTER ]
.SS dcps
.TP
.B dcps.presentation_instance_coherence
How a coherent set is presented under PRESENTATION access_scope INSTANCE. DDS
1.4 words it twice: the table in \(sc2.2.3.6 (p. 106) says "order and coherent
changes apply to each instance separately", the text below it (p. 115) says
that begin_coherent_change and end_coherent_change have "no effect on how the
subscriber can access the data". They differ for a coherent set within one
instance.
.B per_instance
follows the table: the changes of a set to one instance are presented together
once the set is closed (what the OMG dds-rtps cases Test_CoherentSets_10 and
_19 check);
.B none
follows the text: the samples are presented as they arrive. Default per_instance.
.RB [ ZERODDS_PRESENTATION_INSTANCE_COHERENCE ]
.SS writer
.TP
.B writer.nack_response_delay_ms
How long a reliable writer waits after a NACK before it repairs, ms,
>= 0 (DDSI-RTPS 2.5 \(sc8.4.7.1 nackResponseDelay). Default 200, the
specification's; 0 answers on the next tick.
.RB [ ZERODDS_NACK_RESPONSE_DELAY_MS ]
.TP
.B writer.heartbeat_period_ms
How often a reliable writer announces its history to a reader that has not
acknowledged it, ms, > 0 (DDSI-RTPS 2.5 \(sc8.4.7.1 heartbeatPeriod). Default
100.
.RB [ ZERODDS_WRITER_HEARTBEAT_PERIOD_MS ]
.TP
.B writer.nack_suppression_duration_ms
A NACK for a change sent less than this long ago is ignored, ms, >= 0
(DDSI-RTPS 2.5 \(sc8.4.7.1 nackSuppressionDuration). Default 0.
.RB [ ZERODDS_NACK_SUPPRESSION_DURATION_MS ]
.TP
.B writer.unacked_window_bytes
Flow control of a writer with reliable readers: how many bytes of
payload may be written and not yet acknowledged by all of them, >= 0.
Beyond it a write waits for acknowledgements up to the RELIABILITY
max_blocking_time, then goes out past it; TIMEOUT comes only from the
RESOURCE_LIMITS. 0 sets no window. Default 524288.
.RB [ ZERODDS_WRITER_UNACKED_WINDOW ]
.TP
.B writer.unregister_on_delete
Whether deleting a DataWriter unregisters its instances on the wire and,
with WRITER_DATA_LIFECYCLE autodispose_unregistered_instances, disposes
them (DDS 1.4 \(sc2.2.2.4.1.6, \(sc2.2.3.21).
.B false
sends neither: readers take the instances to NOT_ALIVE_NO_WRITERS when the
publication's SEDP dispose arrives, as with Connext, Fast DDS, CoreDX and
HDDS as the publisher (OMG dds-rtps Test_FinalInstanceState_2). Default
true.
.RB [ ZERODDS_WRITER_UNREGISTER_ON_DELETE ]
.TP
.B writer.linger_ms
How long deleting a reliable DataWriter waits for its reliable readers to
acknowledge what it sent, the unregister and dispose markers of
DDS 1.4 \(sc2.2.2.4.1.6 among it, before the writer goes and its SEDP
dispose is sent. A writer whose readers have acknowledged everything goes at
once. Milliseconds, an integer >= 0;
.B 0
removes the writer at once. Default 1000.
.RB [ ZERODDS_WRITER_LINGER_MS ]
.TP
.B writer.group_coherent_set_pid
Whether a writer of a publisher with PRESENTATION access_scope GROUP puts
PID_GROUP_COHERENT_SET on the DATA of a group coherent set, as DDSI-RTPS 2.5
\(sc8.7.6 requires.
.B false
leaves it out for every reader: HDDS and Connext read group coherent sets
right only without it (OMG dds-rtps Test_CoherentSets_12, _18, _21), and a
ZeroDDS subscriber then no longer knows a set's members. Default true.
.RB [ ZERODDS_WRITER_GROUP_COHERENT_SET_PID ]
.TP
.B writer.group_info_on_wire
Whether a writer of a publisher with PRESENTATION access_scope GROUP sends the
group information of DDSI-RTPS 2.5 \(sc8.7.5 and \(sc8.7.6: PID_GROUP_SEQ_NUM,
PID_WRITER_GROUP_INFO, PID_GROUP_COHERENT_SET and the GroupInfoFlag of
HEARTBEAT and GAP.
.B false
sends each writer's coherent set as under TOPIC, the form HDDS and zzdds send
and Connext, HDDS and zzdds read (OMG dds-rtps Test_CoherentSets_12, _18, _21);
a ZeroDDS subscriber then orders by writer instead of by group. Default true.
.RB [ ZERODDS_WRITER_GROUP_INFO_ON_WIRE ]
.TP
.B writer.keyless_lifecycle_on_wire
Send the dispose and unregister of a writer of a topic without a key on the
wire. DDSI-RTPS 2.5 \(sc8.2.10.1 gives both "no effect if the topicKind==NO_KEY":
by default the writer's own instance state changes (DDS 1.4 \(sc2.2.1.2.2, one
instance) and no marker is sent, and
.B DcpsRuntime::keyless_lifecycle_not_sent
counts it.
.B true
sends the marker (status bits only) for a peer that expects it. Default false.
.RB [ ZERODDS_WRITER_KEYLESS_LIFECYCLE_ON_WIRE ]
.SS discovery
.TP
.B discovery.spdp_multicast_group
SPDP multicast group. Default 239.255.0.1.
.RB [ ZERODDS_SPDP_MC_GROUP ]
.TP
.B discovery.spdp_period_ms
SPDP announce period, ms, > 0 (DDSI-RTPS 2.5 \(sc9.6.2.4). Default 30000, the
specification's; a lease shorter than 90 s lowers the default to a third of it.
.RB [ ZERODDS_SPDP_PERIOD_MS ]
.TP
.B discovery.initial_announce_count
Announcements at start, and directed responses to a newly discovered
participant, at the interval below; 0 turns the announcements off and leaves
one response. Default 10.
.RB [ ZERODDS_INITIAL_ANNOUNCE_COUNT ]
.TP
.B discovery.initial_announce_period_ms
Interval of those announcements and responses, ms, > 0. Default 200.
.RB [ ZERODDS_INITIAL_ANNOUNCE_PERIOD_MS ]
.TP
.B discovery.participant_lease_duration_ms
Announced participant lease, ms, > 0. Default 100000.
.RB [ ZERODDS_PARTICIPANT_LEASE_MS ]
.TP
.B discovery.wlp_period_ms
WLP period, ms; 0 is a third of the lease. Default 0.
.RB [ ZERODDS_WLP_PERIOD_MS ]
.TP
.B discovery.spdp_new_sequence_per_period
Whether every SPDP announcement carries a new sequence number. DDSI-RTPS 2.5
\(sc8.5.3.1 has the SPDP writer resend one data-object each period, so by
default the number changes only with the participant data. Cyclone DDS and
OpenDDS send a new number each period;
.B true
does the same. Default false.
.RB [ ZERODDS_SPDP_NEW_SEQUENCE_PER_PERIOD ]
.TP
.B discovery.peers
List of unicast peers,
.IR ipv4 " or " ipv4:port ;
without a port the ports of the domain are expanded. Default empty.
.RB [ ZERODDS_PEERS ]
.TP
.B discovery.participant_name
The name the participant announces as PID_ENTITY_NAME (EntityName_t,
DDSI-RTPS 2.5 Table 9.18). A non-empty string. Default
.B zerodds:
and the GUID prefix in hex. The endpoints of the participant announce a name
built from kind, topic and entity id.
.RB [ ZERODDS_PARTICIPANT_NAME ]
.TP
.B discovery.max_peer_participants
Participant ids per peer in that expansion, 1..=120. Default 10.
.RB [ ZERODDS_MAX_PEER_PARTICIPANTS ]
.TP
.B discovery.no_multicast
Send no SPDP multicast; discovery by
.B discovery.peers
only. Default false.
.RB [ ZERODDS_NO_MULTICAST ]
.TP
.B discovery.type_object_v1
Announce the type also as the TypeObjectV1 (type) and the TypeIdV1 (type_id) of
XTypes 1.1 Annex B, the optional members of the built-in topic data of XTypes
1.3 Annex D. Default false.
.RB [ ZERODDS_TYPE_OBJECT_V1 ]
.TP
.B discovery.multicast_allowlist
List of the multicast groups allowed; empty allows every group. Default
empty.
.RB [ ZERODDS_MULTICAST_ALLOWLIST ]
.TP
.B discovery.server
The participant relays SPDP for others. Default false.
.RB [ ZERODDS_DISCOVERY_SERVER ]
.TP
.B discovery.domain_tag
The domain tag (DDSI-RTPS 2.5 \(sc8.5.3.2): sent in SPDP when not empty; a
participant whose tag differs, the untagged one included, is not
discovered. Cyclone DDS sets it with
.IR Discovery/Tag .
Default empty.
.RB [ ZERODDS_DOMAIN_TAG ]
.SS ports
The port-mapping parameters of DDSI-RTPS 2.5 section 9.6.2.3, which an
implementation must expose.
SPDP multicast port = PB + DG * domainId + d0;
SPDP unicast port = PB + DG * domainId + d1 + PG * participantId;
user-traffic multicast port = PB + DG * domainId + d2;
user-traffic unicast port = PB + DG * domainId + d3 + PG * participantId.
Each value is an integer in 0..=65535.
The runtime binds the discovery ports from them and, with
.BR transport.user_multicast ,
the user-traffic multicast port; its user-traffic unicast sockets take an
ephemeral port, which the participant announces, so d3 is carried in the
mapping without being bound.
Peers that use other values have to be told the same values.
.TP
.B ports.port_base
PB. Default 7400.
.RB [ ZERODDS_PORT_BASE ]
.TP
.B ports.domain_gain
DG. Default 250.
.RB [ ZERODDS_PORT_DOMAIN_GAIN ]
.TP
.B ports.participant_gain
PG. Default 2.
.RB [ ZERODDS_PORT_PARTICIPANT_GAIN ]
.TP
.B ports.d0
Default 0.
.RB [ ZERODDS_PORT_D0 ]
.TP
.B ports.d1
Default 10.
.RB [ ZERODDS_PORT_D1 ]
.TP
.B ports.d2
Default 1.
.RB [ ZERODDS_PORT_D2 ]
.TP
.B ports.d3
Default 11.
.RB [ ZERODDS_PORT_D3 ]
.SS security
.TP
.B security.secure_spdp
The reliable secure SPDP channel (entity 0xff0101), which a secured
participant announces and Fast DDS waits for before it matches secure
endpoints. Runs on a secured participant. Default true.
.RB [ ZERODDS_SECURE_SPDP ]
.TP
.B security.enclave_dir
SROS2 enclave directory
.RI ( identity_ca.cert.pem ,
.IR cert.pem ,
.IR key.pem ,
.IR permissions_ca.cert.pem ,
.IR governance.p7s ,
.IR permissions.p7s );
set, every participant is secured from it, and a directory that does not
load is an error. Default unset.
.RB [ ZERODDS_SECURITY_DIR ]
.PP
The following five keys are the named settings of the security plugins for the
forms in which a peer writes or reads the wire differently from DDS\-Security
1.2. Each default is the value of the specification; the other values are for
one named peer. Participants created from the participant's
.B dds.sec.*
properties may name the same setting as a property
.RB ( org.zerodds.sec.* ),
which wins over the key. A value outside the list stops the participant at
start \(em never a silent default.
.TP
.B security.kx_key_fallback
.BR spec " or " accept :
whether a submessage or payload that no key of its sender opens is also tried
with the key exchange (Kx) key of the volatile channel. DDS\-Security 1.2
10.5.2.1.2 gives that key to the volatile channel only, so spec is the default;
accept is for a peer that protects discovery or user data with it. No vendor
is named: the form is not measured against one. Default spec.
.RB [ ZERODDS_SEC_KX_KEY_FALLBACK ]
.TP
.B security.compatibility_check
.BR spec " or " ignore :
whether participants and endpoints whose announced security masks and
algorithm infos are not compatible are kept apart (DDS\-Security 1.2 7.3.10 to
7.3.24). ignore is for a peer whose announced masks differ from what it
enforces. Default spec.
.RB [ ZERODDS_SEC_COMPATIBILITY_CHECK ]
.TP
.B security.algo_name_nul
.BR spec ", " omit ", " mirror " or " by_vendor :
how the algorithm names c.dsign_algo and c.kagree_algo of the handshake
token are written. DDS\-Security 1.2 7.3.3.1 counts the terminating NUL in the
length (spec). Fast DDS 3.6 writes and compares the names without it: omit
never writes it; mirror writes it as an initiator and answers in the form of the
request. OpenDDS requires the NUL. by_vendor writes it for an OpenDDS peer only
(the form ZeroDDS had before spec was the default). Fast DDS and OpenDDS
behaviour is from the sources read. Default spec.
.RB [ ZERODDS_SEC_ALGO_NAME_NUL ]
.TP
.B security.algorithm_names
.BR auto ", " 1.2 " or " 1.1 :
the spelling of the algorithm names the initiator sends. DDS\-Security 1.2
Tables 25 and 26 name ECDSA+P256+SHA256, ECDHE\-CEUM+P256 and
DHE+MODP\-2048\-256 and mark the names of 1.1 (ECDSA\-SHA256,
ECDH+prime256v1\-CEUM, DH+MODP\-2048\-256) "deprecated in v 1.2"; 1.2 is the
default. Cyclone DDS, Fast DDS and OpenDDS announce DDS:Auth:PKI\-DH:1.0 and
compare with the names of 1.1 (sources read, not measured), so 1.1 is for these
three. auto sends the names of 1.2 to a peer whose IdentityToken names 1.2 and
those of 1.1 to a peer of 1.0. Both spellings are accepted when received.
Default 1.2.
.RB [ ZERODDS_SEC_ALGORITHM_NAMES ]
.TP
.B security.subject_name_form
.BR rfc4514 ", " x509_name_oneline " or " xn_flag_oneline :
the form of the subject names in dds.cert.sn and dds.ca.sn of the IdentityToken
and dds.perm_ca.sn of the PermissionsToken. DDS\-Security 1.2 9.4.1.2.3 gives
RFC 4514 (CN=y,O=x,C=DE), the default. x509_name_oneline is the OpenSSL
X509_NAME_oneline form (/C=DE/O=x/CN=y): Fast DDS 3.6 compares a received
dds.perm_ca.sn with it, and Cyclone DDS writes it. xn_flag_oneline is the
OpenSSL XN_FLAG_ONELINE form (C = DE, O = x, CN = y): OpenDDS compares a
received dds.ca.sn with it. The comparisons are from the sources read; ZeroDDS
compares no received subject name. Default rfc4514.
.RB [ ZERODDS_SEC_SUBJECT_NAME_FORM ]
.TP
.B security.origin_authentication_fallback
.BR spec " or " accept :
what a participant whose governance asks for origin authentication
(rtps_protection_kind or metadata_protection_kind
SIGN_WITH_ORIGIN_AUTHENTICATION or ENCRYPT_WITH_ORIGIN_AUTHENTICATION) does
with an RTPS message or submessage under a key without a receiver\-specific
key. spec refuses it, as DDS\-Security 1.2 10.5.3.3 Table 80
decode_rtps_message asks; accept verifies it with the common MAC alone, as
Table 80 does for a key whose receiver_specific_key_id is zero. A key that
carries a receiver\-specific key is held to it under either value. OpenDDS 3.34
makes no receiver\-specific keys although its governance asks for them
(CryptoBuiltInImpl.cpp: "origin auth is not implemented"). Default spec.
.RB [ ZERODDS_SEC_ORIGIN_AUTHENTICATION_FALLBACK ]
.SS tsn
Read when
.B transport.user_transport
is
.BR TSN .
.TP
.B tsn.interface
Network interface. Default unset.
.RB [ ZERODDS_TSN_IFACE ]
.TP
.B tsn.vlan
VLAN id, 0..=4094. Default 0.
.RB [ ZERODDS_TSN_VLAN ]
.TP
.B tsn.pcp
Priority code point, 0..=7. Default 0.
.RB [ ZERODDS_TSN_PCP ]
.SS threads
Real-time scheduling and CPU affinity for the receive threads (SPDP
multicast, metatraffic, user data, the extra receive workers) as one group
and the tick thread as another. Applied through
.IR zerodds\-rt\-linux ,
Linux
.BR sched_setattr (2)/
.BR sched_setaffinity (2);
needs a build with the
.B rt
feature. Without that feature, or on a non-Linux target, any of these keys
other than the absent key stops the program at start \(em never a silent
run on the default scheduler. A CPU index that is not one of the host's own
CPUs is the same kind of error, never silently dropped from the mask.
.TP
.B threads.recv_policy
Scheduler class of the receive threads:
.BR other ", " fifo ", " rr ", "
or
.BR deadline .
Unset with
.B threads.recv_priority
set keeps the pre-C2(2) meaning,
.BR fifo .
Unset with nothing else in this group set either: no real-time scheduling
and no affinity, exactly as before this key existed. A priority set with
.B other
or
.BR deadline ,
a
.B threads.recv_deadline
set with anything but
.BR deadline ,
.B fifo
or
.B rr
without a priority, and
.B deadline
without
.B threads.recv_deadline
are each a startup error naming the key at fault. Default unset.
.RB [ ZERODDS_RECV_THREAD_POLICY ]
.TP
.B threads.tick_policy
Like
.BR threads.recv_policy ,
for the tick thread.
.RB [ ZERODDS_TICK_THREAD_POLICY ]
.TP
.B threads.recv_priority
.B sched_priority
of the receive threads under
.B fifo
or
.BR rr ,
1..=99; needs
.B CAP_SYS_NICE
or
.BR RLIMIT_RTPRIO .
Default unset.
.RB [ ZERODDS_RECV_THREAD_PRIORITY ]
.TP
.B threads.tick_priority
Like
.BR threads.recv_priority ,
for the tick thread.
.RB [ ZERODDS_TICK_THREAD_PRIORITY ]
.TP
.B threads.recv_deadline
.BR SCHED_DEADLINE " (" threads.recv_policy = deadline )
reservation of the receive threads, as
.IR runtime , deadline , period
in nanoseconds,
.IR runtime " <= " deadline " <= " period .
Always needs
.BR CAP_SYS_NICE ;
a reservation that exceeds the kernel's global bandwidth limit fails with
.BR EBUSY .
Default unset.
.RB [ ZERODDS_RECV_THREAD_DEADLINE ]
.TP
.B threads.tick_deadline
Like
.BR threads.recv_deadline ,
for the tick thread.
.RB [ ZERODDS_TICK_THREAD_DEADLINE ]
.TP
.B threads.recv_cpus
List of CPU indices the receive threads run on. Default unset.
.RB [ ZERODDS_RECV_THREAD_CPUS ]
.TP
.B threads.tick_cpus
List of CPU indices the tick thread runs on. Default unset.
.RB [ ZERODDS_TICK_THREAD_CPUS ]
.TP
.B threads.extra_recv
Additional receive workers, 0..=64. Default 0.
.RB [ ZERODDS_EXTRA_RECV_THREADS ]
.SS wire
.TP
.B wire.rtps_dialect
Behaviour table for vendor differences:
.BR cyclone ", " fastdds ", " opendds ", " maximally\-interoperable .
Default maximally-interoperable.
.RB [ ZERODDS_RTPS_DIALECT ]
.TP
.B wire.codec_dialect
CDR dialect of the reflective codecs: the body check and key derivation for
the language bindings, the webdds bridge, the ROS 2 dynamic type support, and
the default of \fBzerodds\-spy\fR and \fBzerodds\-record \-\-dialect\fR.
The names \fBzerodds\-idlc generate \-\-dialect\fR takes:
.BR specification ", " cyclone ", " rti ", " fastdds ", " opendds ", " omniorb ,
.BR tao ", " jacorb ", " ros\-fastrtps ", " ros\-cyclone ", " opensplice .
Set it to the dialect the types were generated in. Default specification.
.RB [ ZERODDS_CODEC_DIALECT ]
.TP
.B wire.encapsulation_ids
Identifiers of the encapsulation header of a user sample.
.B table\-60
(default) sends those of DDS\-XTypes 1.3 Table 60 (\(sc7.6.3.1.2): 0x0006 to 0x000b for
XCDR2 and 0x0004 for XML, which Cyclone DDS, Fast DDS, OpenDDS and RTI Connext send.
.B table\-39
sends those of Table 39 (\(sc7.4.3.4): 0x0010 (PLAIN_CDR2 big endian) to 0x0015
(DELIMIT_CDR little endian) and {0x01, 0x00} for XML. The XCDR2 identifiers are those of
DDSI\-RTPS 2.5 Table 10.3. Both tables are read. XCDR1 identifiers do not change.
.RB [ ZERODDS_WIRE_ENCAPSULATION_IDS ]
.TP
.B wire.pl_cdr1_headers
PL_CDR1 header policy of the reflective codecs, in place of the one of
.BR wire.codec_dialect .
.B dialect
(default) keeps the dialect's. The names:
.BR specification ", " short\-rounded\-length ", " always\-extended ", " short\-true\-length ,
.BR extended\-for\-constructed ", " extended\-for\-constructed\-in\-large\-samples .
\fBspecification\fR writes and expects the exact parameter length of the member (DDS\-XTypes 1.3
\(sc7.4.1.2); \fBshort\-rounded\-length\fR the length rounded up to four (DDSI\-RTPS 2.5
\(sc9.4.2.11). The setting holds for the whole process.
.RB [ ZERODDS_WIRE_PL_CDR1_HEADERS ]
.TP
.B wire.pl_cdr1_reading
.B strict
(default): the reflective PL_CDR1 reader takes the framing it is configured for and reports the
flags, header form and length convention of another framing. \fBtolerant\fR: it accepts any
flags on PID_EXTENDED and PID_LIST_END, the short and the extended header and a length that is
the member's own or rounded up to four (DDS\-XTypes 1.3 \(sc7.4.1.2.1, \(sc7.4.1.2.2). The
setting holds for the whole process.
.RB [ ZERODDS_WIRE_PL_CDR1_READING ]
.TP
.B wire.type_lookup_encoding
The encoding of the requests and replies of the TypeLookup service.
.B xcdr2
(default): CDR2_LE {0x00,0x07} with the encoding version 2. DDS\-XTypes 1.4 Beta 1
\(sc7.6.3.3.3 makes it the data representation of these endpoints (OMG issue DDSXTY14\-29).
Cyclone DDS 11.0.1, Fast DDS 3.6.1, OpenDDS 3.34.0, RTI Connext 7.7.0 and InterCom DDS 4.3.1
write CDR2_LE.
.B xcdr1
writes CDR_LE {0x00,0x01} with the encoding version 1, the reading of DDS\-XTypes 1.3, which
names no data representation for these endpoints (\(sc7.6.3.1.1, Table 60), and of DDSI\-RTPS
2.5 \(sc10.4 Table 10.2. Cyclone DDS, OpenDDS and RTI Connext refuse a CDR_LE request and
reply, Fast DDS answers a CDR_LE request (measured).
A received sample is read in the encoding its identifier names, in either setting.
.RB [ ZERODDS_WIRE_TYPE_LOOKUP_ENCODING ]
.TP
.B wire.data_representation_offer
List of the offered data representations,
.BR XCDR1 " (also " XCDR ", " 1 )
and
.BR XCDR2 " (also " 2 );
the first is the encoding of the writers. Default XCDR1 (XTypes 1.3 7.6.3.1.1).
A reader requests XCDR2 and XCDR beside it (XTypes 1.3 7.6.3.1.1, readers from XTYPES 1.2 on).
.RB [ ZERODDS_DATA_REPR_OFFER ]
.TP
.B wire.data_representation_match
.BR strict " or " tolerant
matching of the data representation: strict, the clause (XTypes 1.3 7.6.3.1.1),
requires the writer's offered representation in the reader's list; tolerant matches on
any overlap, as Cyclone DDS and Fast DDS do. Default strict.
.RB [ ZERODDS_DATA_REPR_MATCH ]
.TP
.B wire.big_endian
Writers encode big endian. Default false.
.RB [ ZERODDS_WIRE_BIG_ENDIAN ]
.TP
.B wire.accept_empty_acknack_base_zero
Accept an ACKNACK whose readerSNState is empty (numBits 0) with bitmapBase 0.
DDSI-RTPS 2.5 \(sc9.4.2.6 requires bitmapBase >= 1, so by default such an
ACKNACK is invalid and the rest of its message with it. OpenDDS 3.34.0 and
RTI Connext 7.7.0 send it as the ACKNACK of a reader that holds nothing. The
setting holds for the whole process: one runtime in a process that turns it
on turns it on for every runtime in that process, and it is never turned off
again. Default false.
.RB [ ZERODDS_WIRE_ACCEPT_EMPTY_ACKNACK_BASE_ZERO ]
.TP
.B wire.clock_skew_threshold_s
CLOCK_SKEW_DETECTED of DDSI-RTPS 2.5 \(sc8.3.7.4: the largest difference in
seconds between the time a message is received and the rtpsSendTimestamp of its
HeaderExtension. A message beyond it sets clockSkewDetected in the receiver
state, and the InfoTimestamp after it is moved by the difference
(\(sc8.3.8.11). The spec gives no threshold, so the detection is off by
default: 0 is off.
.RB [ ZERODDS_WIRE_CLOCK_SKEW_THRESHOLD_S ]
.TP
.B wire.omit_heartbeat_group_digests
Leave writerSet and secureWriterSet out of the HEARTBEAT that carries group
information (GroupInfoFlag set). DDSI-RTPS 2.5 Table 8.44 and the reader rule of
\(sc8.7.5 ask for both, and \(sc9.4.2.19 and \(sc9.3.2.5 give their form (four
octets each, the first four octets of the MD5 of the EntityIdSet_t, zero for the
empty group). The figure of \(sc9.4.5.7 ends the HEARTBEAT after lastGSN. With
this setting a writer sends that form. A reader accepts both. Default false.
.RB [ ZERODDS_WIRE_OMIT_HEARTBEAT_GROUP_DIGESTS ]
.TP
.SS time
.TP
.B time.source
The wall clock behind SOURCE_TIMESTAMP, DESTINATION_ORDER=BY_SOURCE_TIMESTAMP,
.I write_w_timestamp
defaults and the reader's LIFESPAN/BY_RECEPTION_TIMESTAMP reception clock
.RI ( crates/dcps/src/time_source.rs ).
.BR system " (default, the platform clock) or " tai
(Linux only,
.IR CLOCK_TAI ;
refused on a build/host that cannot honour it, not a silent fallback to
.IR system ).
A programmatic source
.RI ( TimeServiceClock ", an OMG Time Service 1.1 " zerodds\-time\-service
adapter carrying its inaccuracy/tdf; a deterministic
.I TestClock
for tests; any other
.IR Arc<dyn\ TimeSource> )
is set on
.I RuntimeConfig::time_source
directly — it has no file spelling.
.RB [ ZERODDS_TIME_SOURCE ]
.SS xtypes, durability, delivery
.TP
.B xtypes.key_member_bounds
.BR enforced " or " ignored :
the key-member rules of XTypes 1.3 table 19 in assignability. Default
enforced.
.RB [ ZERODDS_KEY_MEMBER_BOUNDS ]
.TP
.B xtypes.strong_assignability
.BR equivalence " or " names_ignored :
what strong assignability (XTypes 1.3 \(sc7.2.4.3) asks of an element or
member type that is not delimited: equivalence under the MINIMAL relation,
member names included, or, with names_ignored, assignability in both
directions without member names. Default equivalence.
.RB [ ZERODDS_STRONG_ASSIGNABILITY ]
.TP
.B xtypes.type_consistency_absent
.BR disallow " or " allow :
what a remote reader that announces no TypeConsistencyEnforcement (PID 0x0074)
requests. disallow is the clause (XTypes 1.3 7.6.3.4.1); allow takes the
policy's default, ALLOW_TYPE_COERCION, for a peer such as Cyclone DDS that does
not write the PID. Default disallow.
.RB [ ZERODDS_TYPE_CONSISTENCY_ABSENT ]
.TP
.B xtypes.annotation_parameter_order
.BR member\-index " or " name :
the order of the parameters of a CompleteAnnotationType: member\-index, the
declaration order (XTypes 1.3 7.3.4.5), or name, the comment in Annex B. The
order is in the object and so in the hash of annotation_typeid; the setting holds
for the whole process. Default member\-index.
.RB [ ZERODDS_ANNOTATION_PARAMETER_ORDER ]
.TP
.B durability.dir
Root of the store of PERSISTENT writers. Default
.IR <temp\ dir>/zerodds\-durability .
.RB [ ZERODDS_DURABILITY_DIR ]
.TP
.B delivery.mode
.BR portable ", " raw\-same\-host " or " iceoryx :
the delivery path of loaning writers (C API, RMW publishers of loanable
types); where a mode does not apply, a warning names the reason. Default
portable.
.RB [ ZERODDS_DELIVERY_MODE ]
.SS diagnostics
.TP
.B diagnostics.observability
Event sink:
.BR off " (also " none ", " 0 ", " false ", empty), " stderr ,
.BR stderr\-json " (also " json ", " 1 ", " on ", " true ).
Default off.
.RB [ ZERODDS_OBSERVABILITY ]
.TP
.B diagnostics.trace_send_errors
Failed sends on stderr. Default false.
.RB [ ZERODDS_TRACE_SEND_ERR ]
.TP
.B diagnostics.phase_timing
Latency counters per phase. Default false.
.RB [ ZERODDS_PHASE_TIMING ]
.TP
.B diagnostics.phase_dump
Print those counters when the runtime is dropped. Default false.
.RB [ ZERODDS_PHASE_DUMP ]
.SS ros2
.B ros2.*
is the section of
.IR rmw_zerodds_cpp ,
described in
.BR zerodds\-ros2.yaml (5);
other programs leave it to the RMW.
.B ros2.time_based_filter
.RB [ ZERODDS_TIME_BASED_FILTER ]
is read by both.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/zerodds/zerodds.yaml
The base file on Linux.
.TP
.I /etc/zerodds/zerodds.d/
Drop-ins, read in name order after the base.
.TP
.I /run/zerodds/zerodds.d/
Runtime drop-ins, read last (Linux).
.TP
.I /usr/share/zerodds/configs/zerodds.yaml.example
Every key at its default.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
discovery:
  no_multicast: true
  peers:
    \- 192.0.2.20
    \- 192.0.2.21:7410
transport:
  user_transports: [SHM, UDPv4]
.fi
.PP
.nf
zerodds\-spy \-\-set discovery.no_multicast=true \-\-check\-config
.fi
.PP
.nf
threads:
  recv_policy: fifo
  recv_priority: 80
  recv_cpus: [2, 3]
  tick_policy: deadline
  tick_deadline: "1000000,5000000,10000000"
.fi
